1. ApplicationContext
      Think of it as the container that holds all your beans.
      It doesn’t care where the beans come from — it just manages them.

Responsibilities:

      Keeps track of all beans and their dependencies.
      Instantiates beans when the app starts (or lazily if configured).
      Injects dependencies (@Autowired) into beans.
      Calls lifecycle methods (@PostConstruct) on beans.
      Handles events and manages bean scope.

   In short: ApplicationContext = “where all beans live and are managed.”

2. Auto-Configuration
      Auto-configuration is a process that tells Spring Boot which beans to create automatically.
      It is one way beans get added to the ApplicationContext.

Responsibilities:
       Looks at what libraries are present.
       Checks conditions (@ConditionalOnClass, @ConditionalOnMissingBean, etc.).
       Registers beans into the ApplicationContext if conditions pass.

    In short: Auto-configuration = “rules that automatically add beans to the ApplicationContext.”

3. How they work together
       Spring Boot starts and creates an ApplicationContext.
       Spring Boot processes auto-configuration classes.
       Each auto-configuration class may create beans and register them in the ApplicationContext.
    The ApplicationContext now contains:
        our beans (from @Component, @Service, @Controller, @Bean)
        Beans from auto-configuration (like DataSource, DispatcherServlet, etc.)

 applicationContext manages all beans, whether they came from you or auto-configuration.



 1- Maven/Gradle builds the project, downloads all dependencies defined in the build file, and makes them available on the classpath.
 2- When the application starts, Spring Boot creates an ApplicationContext.
 3- The ApplicationContext is responsible for managing all beans.
 4- Auto-configuration checks what classes and libraries are present on the classpath and registers the required beans into the ApplicationContext.
 5- The ApplicationContext then creates those beans and injects dependencies into classes that depend on them.